# Runtime database — generated state, not source. Re-seeded on first
# startup from backend/exported_output_stage3/ (which IS tracked).
backend/gluribridge.db

# Raw scraped data — large, regenerable via backend/scrapers/*, not source.
# Includes the ~9.8GB BRWA legal-decree PDF archive
# (backend/data/raw/brwa_profiles/pdfs/) — confirmed unread by the running
# app (nothing outside backend/scrapers/brwa/brwa_crawler.py itself
# references it; the live app only ever uses the real external pdf_url).
# backend/data/regulatory/ and backend/tests/sample_data/ are NOT under
# this path and ship normally — the regulatory rules are real source data
# compliance.py reads at runtime, and the test fixtures are load-bearing
# for the test suite.
#
# CORRECTION (2026-08-31, found via real 404s in the live Render
# deployment's logs, not caught by local testing): the "confirmed unread"
# reasoning above was only ever verified for the PDF archive specifically
# — the blanket exclusion this rule used to be (a bare backend/data/raw/)
# also swept up two paths app/territories.py DOES read, live, per
# request: brwa_geojson/geojson/*.geojson (real BRWA polygon geometry —
# GET /territories/{idx}/geometry) and brwa_profiles/wa_list.json (the
# full 2,283-row BRWA list — GET /territories search and the /stats
# brwa_territories count). Neither was ever pushed to GitHub, so the live
# deployment silently ran with zero BRWA data: confirmed directly against
# the live API — GET /stats returned brwa_territories:
# {total:0,with_geometry:0} instead of the real {2283,1756}, EVERY
# GET /territories/{idx}/geometry 404'd (not just the genuine ~23% real
# gap), and GET /territories?search= returned []. Un-ignored below —
# 149MB + 1.1MB, real and static (frozen with the rest of this project's
# data through Demo Day), a one-time cost worth paying so the live
# Territory Discovery feature actually works. brwa_profiles/pdfs/
# (9.8GB), brwa_profiles/profiles/ (25MB, confirmed unread by any app/*.py
# — only normalize_brwa.py during an offline pipeline run touches it),
# and brwa_profiles/crawler.log stay excluded, still caught by the
# unnegated backend/data/raw/* below.
backend/data/raw/*
!backend/data/raw/brwa_geojson/
!backend/data/raw/brwa_profiles/
backend/data/raw/brwa_profiles/*
!backend/data/raw/brwa_profiles/wa_list.json

# Raw bulk source material at the project root — the original manual
# downloads (Verra Excel export, SRUK/SRN-PPI project-list zips) that
# predate and seeded the scrapers now in backend/scrapers/*. Same
# reasoning as backend/data/raw/ above: zero code references anywhere
# (grepped .py/.ts/.tsx across the whole repo), not read by the running
# app. Left on disk, just not committed.
GluriDocs/

# Stray manual backup copies from earlier session work — confirmed zero
# code references anywhere (grepped the whole backend), not real source
# or fixtures. Left on disk, just not committed.
backend/data/geojson - Copy/
backend/data/geojson - Copy.zip
backend/data/profiles.zip
backend/data/wa_list.json

# Root-level leftovers, each confirmed zero code references: full backup
# snapshots of package states now superseded by backend/gluribridge/ and
# backend/scrapers/verra/ respectively; a stale dated report snapshot
# (references 140 candidates, the live system now has 144); and
# /brwa_crawler.py, which despite its name isn't code at all — it's a
# pasted terminal transcript (shell prompt + a circular-import traceback)
# accidentally saved with a .py extension.
/gluribridge.zip
/verra_crawler.zip
/brwa_crawler.py
/current_output.md

# Same story, one directory in: full old working-directory snapshots
# (source + stale __pycache__/*.pyc + sample output, all zipped together)
# sitting beside the real, active .py scrapers that superseded them. Zero
# code references.
backend/scrapers/brwa/brwa_crawler.zip
backend/scrapers/srn_ppi/srn_ppi_scraper.zip
backend/scrapers/sruk/sruk_indonesia_scraper.zip

# Regenerates fresh every test_export.py run (export_pipeline_result(...,
# output_dir="exported_output") is a relative path, so it lands wherever
# the CWD happened to be at invocation time) — not a deliberate fixture
# like backend/tests/sample_data/, so committing any of them would just
# create spurious diffs every time the tests run locally. Found two more
# instances of the same artifact beyond the one already flagged:
# backend/exported_output/ is explicitly called "stale" in orchestrate.py's
# own comment (pre-dates the 2026-08-27 reorg); the repo-root one is a
# leftover from an earlier ad hoc run. Real seed data lives in
# backend/exported_output_stage3/ instead, which is NOT matched by these
# patterns and ships normally.
backend/tests/exported_output/
backend/exported_output/
/exported_output/

# Python
gluri_env/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/

# Node
node_modules/
dist/
dist-ssr/
*.local

# Env / secrets — none exist in this repo today; ignored preemptively so
# a future local .env is never committed by accident.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
secrets.json

# Legacy vanilla-JS frontend — kept during the React rebuild purely as a
# functional-diff comparison baseline. That migration is complete and
# re-verified (feature parity confirmed across this session); frontend-react/
# is the real product going forward. Real, working code, not junk — a
# deliberate decision, not a "same as the backups above" case. Left on disk.
/frontend/

# Ad hoc dev-iteration content — screenshots from this session's visual
# reviews (not a sanctioned test-verification output like
# frontend/screenshots/ was; most show now-superseded UI states) and
# anything else that lands here, like the file-list this exact .gitignore
# review generated. The whole directory, not just screenshots/ — "tmp"
# means throwaway, consistently. Left on disk as a local record.
/tmp/

# OS / editor cruft
.DS_Store
# Windows/WSL alternate-data-stream artifact left behind when a
# browser-downloaded file is copied over from Windows — pure metadata
# cruft, not real data (found 3 of these under backend/data/regulatory/).
*:Zone.Identifier
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
.idea/

# Claude Code's own local tool-runtime state (e.g. scheduled_tasks.lock —
# a session-id/PID/timestamp lock file created by this session's own
# background-task scheduling) — local machine/session state, zero project
# relevance, confirmed safe.
.claude/
